.TH IPTABLES\-ANALYZER 1 "2026-11-09" "0.6.0" "iptables-analyzer manual"
.SH NAME
iptables\-analyzer \- analyze iptables/ip6tables firewall rules for security issues
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iptables\-analyzer
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iptables\-analyzer
inspects iptables and ip6tables rules to identify security weaknesses, including
shadowed or ineffective rules, Docker NAT bypasses that make INPUT rules useless,
DOCKER\-USER and FORWARD rules that never match published traffic, containers
reachable by direct routing, services exposed to the network, blocked IPv6
neighbor discovery, and missing best practices. It provides a security score,
actionable recommendations, and a recommended baseline firewall that is always
generated for both IPv4 and IPv6. The grade is marked INCOMPLETE
when listening-service evidence or a firewall translation could not be assessed.
.PP
When run as root without any file arguments, it reads rules directly from the
live system. Otherwise, provide saved rule files with \fB\-f\fR and \fB\-\-file6\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " \fIFILE\fR"
Path to iptables-save output file (IPv4 rules).
.TP
.BR \-\-file6 " \fIFILE\fR"
Path to ip6tables-save output file (IPv6 rules).
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-\-ipv4\-only
Analyze only IPv4 rules.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6\-only
Analyze only IPv6 rules.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output.
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Output results as JSON.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show all rules including empty chains and the original raw rule text.
.TP
.BR \-\-services\-file " \fIFILE\fR"
Path to saved
.BR ss (8)
output file (alternative to live service detection).
An explicitly requested file that is missing or malformed is a fatal error.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-score\-only
Only print the security score. The output includes assessment completeness.
.TP
.BR \-\-baseline "[=\fIFAMILY\fR]"
Print only the recommended baseline firewall in iptables\-restore format and
exit. \fIFAMILY\fR is \fB4\fR, \fB6\fR or \fBall\fR (the default). A single
family is printed alone so it can be redirected into a rules file. The value
must be attached with \fB=\fR. Without rule files and without root the baseline
is generic; otherwise it keeps the detected SSH port, lists other listeners as
commented rules and, on Docker hosts, adds DOCKER\-USER rules for published ports.
Apply it with \fBiptables\-restore \-\-noflush\fR, which keeps Docker's chains.
.TP
.BR \-\-sandbox " \fIMODE\fR"
Confine the process after it has read its input; see
.BR SECURITY .
\fIMODE\fR is \fBauto\fR (the default: apply what the system supports),
\fBstrict\fR (fail unless the whole sandbox is in effect) or \fBoff\fR.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run live analysis as root (auto-detects rules and listening services):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-analyzer
.EE
.RE
.PP
Analyze saved rule files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
iptables\-analyzer \-f rules.v4 \-\-file6 rules.v6
.EE
.RE
.PP
Analyze saved rules with saved service data:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
iptables\-analyzer \-f rules.v4 \-\-services\-file services.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output only the security score:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-analyzer \-s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the recommended baselines to files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-analyzer \-\-baseline=4 > baseline.v4
sudo iptables\-analyzer \-\-baseline=6 > baseline.v6
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save rules for offline analysis:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-save > rules.v4
sudo ip6tables\-save > rules.v6
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save listening services for offline analysis:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ss \-tulnp > services.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Rule files, saved
.BR ss (8)
output and the live tool output are untrusted input: comments, chain names,
interface names and process names are chosen by whoever controls the firewall
or runs a process. The analyzer therefore:
.IP \(bu 2
reads at most 64 MiB of rules and 16 MiB of
.BR ss (8)
output;
.IP \(bu 2
in live mode, runs only root-owned, not group- or world-writable
.BR iptables\-save ,
.BR ip6tables\-save " and " ss
from /usr/sbin, /usr/bin, /sbin or /bin (PATH is a fallback with the same
checks), with an environment that contains only PATH and LC_ALL=C;
.IP \(bu 2
after reading its input, drops root privileges to uid and gid 65534, sets
no_new_privs, and enforces a Landlock domain that denies all file access,
execution, TCP and UDP, abstract UNIX sockets and signals (as far as the
kernel's Landlock ABI supports them). Parsing, analysis and output run in this
sandbox. Landlock needs Linux 5.13 or later with landlock in the lsm= boot
parameter; binaries built with cgo need Linux 7.0 (Landlock ABI 8);
.IP \(bu 2
shows control characters, bidirectional overrides and invalid UTF\-8 in
input text as visible placeholders (\fB^[\fR, \fB<U+202E>\fR,
\fB<0xFF>\fR);
.IP \(bu 2
quotes every value from the ruleset in recommended commands, so that copying
a command into a shell cannot run a command hidden in a rule comment.
.SH SEE ALSO
